= Book Matter =

'''Book Matter is the recognizable anatomy of knowledge within an article: the distinct kinds of explanation, evidence, interpretation, and reference material that together make a subject intelligible.''' It gives authors, readers, and agents a language for selecting the part of an account they need without reducing the account to disconnected fragments.

Its purpose is '''composable knowledge with coherent meaning'''. A complete article should be understandable as a whole; a useful portion may also deserve independent identification, retrieval, comparison, or reuse.

Book Matter describes the '''role of content''', not the kind of entity being discussed. A Method can be the subject of an article, and that article can also contain a ''Methods'' section. These are different meanings of the word.

