A Book Matter role can exist as an ordinary section inside a page. It is '''not automatically''' a category, namespace, semantic property, subpage, or separately published article.

== A practical vocabulary of knowledge components ==

The following names describe recurring functions of knowledge content. Each is a distinct reusable role, not a mandatory heading and not a child of a rigid universal hierarchy. Closely related roles can be combined where that improves reading.

{| class="wikitable"
! Matter !! Intellectual job
|-
| '''Infobox''' || Present a compact, clearly scoped summary of identifying attributes; it is a display device, not evidence in itself.
|-
| '''Overview''' || Introduce the subject and orient the reader to its significance.
|-
| '''Definition''' || Establish the meaning of a term, phenomenon, or object, including multiple legitimate senses where needed.
|-
| '''Scope''' || State boundaries, assumptions, exclusions, contexts, and why they matter.
|-
| '''Synopsis''' || Condense the structure or argument of a larger work without replacing the full treatment.
|-
| '''Foundations''' || Explain underlying principles, premises, and prerequisite knowledge.
|-
| '''Key ideas''' || Identify and connect the central conceptual distinctions.
|-
| '''Theory''' || Present explanatory or predictive accounts, assumptions, and rival formulations.
|-
| '''Framework''' || Organize a set of concepts or methods into a coherent analytic structure.
|-
| '''Mechanism''' || Describe processes or interactions that produce an outcome where mechanistic explanation is applicable.
|-
| '''Methodology''' || Explain how inquiry, analysis, design, or evaluation is conducted.
|-
| '''Implementation''' || Describe a particular realization, system, procedure, or technical artifact.
|-
| '''Examples''' || Make abstract claims understandable through representative instances.
|-
| '''Applications''' || Show how an idea, technique, or finding is used, and in which conditions.
|-
| '''Case studies''' || Examine a specific instance in its context, including consequences and limitations.
|-
| '''Evidence''' || Present observations, records, results, arguments, or proofs bearing on a particular claim.
|-
| '''Data and visualizations''' || Organize datasets, figures, diagrams, tables, and their interpretive context.
|-
| '''Lists''' || Enumerate relevant items with clear selection, ordering, and inclusion criteria.
|-
| '''Comparisons''' || Distinguish neighboring concepts, competing methods, systems, or interpretations.
|-
| '''Criticism and debate''' || Represent substantive objections, controversies, and their respective support.
|-
| '''Limitations''' || Explain uncertainty, failure modes, boundaries, and unresolved problems.
|-
| '''Historical context''' || Describe origins, influences, prior interpretations, and relevant intellectual lineage.
|-
| '''Timeline''' || Present developments and events in meaningful chronological order.
|-
| '''Glossary''' || Define specialized terms and disambiguate recurring vocabulary.
|-
| '''Questions and answers''' || Resolve common misunderstandings or recurring questions efficiently.
|-
| '''Outline or index''' || Provide structural orientation and routes to specific information.
|-
| '''Multimedia''' || Supply meaningful imagery, audio, video, or interactive representation where permitted and useful.
|-
| '''Notes''' || Preserve clarifications, caveats, annotations, and technical qualifications without interrupting the main exposition.
|-
| '''Contributors and acknowledgements''' || Credit significant intellectual contributions and assistance where appropriate, without replacing revision history.
|-
| '''Discussion''' || Summarize substantive unresolved questions or competing arguments; informal conversation threads remain separate from the canonical account.
|-
| '''Editorial review''' || Make consequential corrections or review status intelligible where public disclosure is warranted; routine maintenance records belong in the appropriate project or history surfaces.
|-
| '''References and bibliography''' || Identify works and records that support, contextualize, or historically inform the account.
|-
| '''Further reading''' || Recommend sources for deeper or alternative study, distinguished from works cited as evidence.
|-
| '''Related subjects''' || Connect the reader to genuinely relevant articles, methods, or conceptual neighbors.
|-
| '''Appendices''' || Hold supplementary detail that is useful but would interrupt the main presentation.
|}

These are functional names, not a claim that BITwiki has a corresponding template, property, or register for every role. A subject may need only a few roles. An experimental study, philosophical argument, biography, and mathematical theorem should not be made to share identical section requirements.

== Knowledge architecture without compulsory fragmentation ==

An article on a scientific phenomenon might need an overview, mechanisms, evidence, disputed explanations, applications, and references. An article on a historical event might instead need a chronology, primary sources, conflicting interpretations, and consequences.

'''Do not create empty sections to satisfy a checklist.''' Do not invent a mechanism for a subject that does not have one, or force a computational interpretation merely because information and computation are important perspectives in BITwiki.

Content should be divided where that makes its reasoning, source support, or navigation clearer. A heading may organize material well without justifying an independent page.

== When a component needs its own identity ==

A portion may be worth independently addressing when there is a demonstrable reason, such as:

* a second article or view needs the same authoritative explanation;
* the component is sufficiently substantial to carry its own sources and limitations;
* distinct authors or reviews maintain the material on a different cadence;
* a reader or agent has a recurring need to retrieve the portion independently;
* separate maintenance reduces duplication and source drift;
* one stable component contributes legitimately to multiple disciplinary treatments.

These are considerations, not a requirement to extract material as soon as one condition appears. Separate a fragment only when its meaning, provenance, and editorial responsibility remain understandable outside the original paragraph.

A reusable definition is often a better candidate than a context-dependent conclusion whose meaning changes when surrounding qualifications are removed.

== Canonical authorship and reuse ==

A '''canonical source''' is the place where a piece of content is intentionally maintained. Its reuse elsewhere should preserve the source's attribution, relevant evidence, uncertainty and scope.

<pre>
subject article
    ├─ explanation written for that subject
    ├─ references and claim qualifications
    └─ carefully selected reusable matter
                    ↓
        another legitimate reading context
</pre>

The complete article remains an intelligible reading surface even if it incorporates material maintained elsewhere. The reused content must not become context-free or acquire stronger epistemic standing merely because its host article appears authoritative.

[[BITwiki:Transclusion]] explains when and how the MediaWiki source can actually be incorporated by reference. The existence of a Book Matter role alone does not create a transclusion, semantic identity, or machine-addressable fragment.

== Relation to Simple, Core, and Technical ==

Reading depth answers '''how the same subject is explained to different audiences'''. Book Matter answers '''what intellectual function a portion of the explanation performs'''.

A Core definition and a Simple definition may communicate the same underlying meaning at different levels of detail. It is not necessarily useful to transclude the Core wording verbatim into Simple. Similarly, a Technical treatment may need separate formalism while preserving the same essential claim and provenance.

Share source material or structured representations where doing so genuinely helps consistency; allow different explanations when pedagogy requires them. Cross-depth consistency is an editorial obligation, not an automatic property of transclusion.

== Current implementation boundaries ==

BITwiki's source repository demonstrates routine use of [[Template:Knowledge object]], [[Template:Reading depth]], and portal templates. It also contains a small number of other slash-titled Main pages, but it does '''not''' establish a broadly deployed, independently addressable Book Matter registry or universal retrieval API for each role listed above.

Ordinary headings, page titles and source text are available now. More sophisticated component identity, retrieval projections, dependency indexing, or agent-context assembly would require explicit definitions and runtime verification.

Before introducing a fragment identifier or new component schema, decide what it identifies, how revisions affect it, where annotations belong, and whether reuse warrants an actual page rather than a section.

== Editorial tests ==

A useful component should answer four questions:

# '''Purpose:''' What specific understanding or evidentiary function does it serve?
# '''Context:''' What must accompany it to prevent misinterpretation?
# '''Source:''' Where are the material claims, attribution and qualifications maintained?
# '''Reuse:''' Does independent access improve the article or merely add another layer to maintain?

The preferred result is not the largest possible set of modules. It is a system in which complex knowledge remains accessible, composable, traceable and intellectually intact.

